- preview_of
- deprecated_by
- commonly_compared_with

## 9. AI Model Decision Rules for QUANDA

### Do not recommend a model merely because it is powerful
- Prefer the smallest / cheapest / simplest model or non-AI method that satisfies the task.
- If deterministic code solves the task reliably, prefer deterministic code.
- If the user already has access to a suitable model, switching providers should incur friction cost.
- If a local model satisfies privacy and quality requirements, do not force a hosted route.
- If a hosted API meaningfully reduces setup compared with self-hosting, include that advantage explicitly.
- If a project needs image / screenshot understanding, require multimodal support.
- If a project needs repository edits, require an agent/tool environment capable of actually editing and validating code rather than assuming raw chat access is equivalent.
- Treat model benchmark claims as evidence inputs, not final project proof.
- Test candidate models on representative project tasks where model choice materially affects project risk.

### Proof requirements
For each AI-model recommendation, QUANDA should be able to answer:
- Why is AI needed for this task?
- Why this model family?
- Why this access route?
- Why hosted or local?
- Why not the other top candidate?
- What additional setup does this route introduce?
- What cost/privacy/license risk exists?
- How will the user validate the output?

### Alternative-path evidence
When AI model choice materially changes the pipeline, include alternate paths such as:
- hosted frontier model
- lower-cost hosted model
- open-weight hosted model
- local model
- deterministic / no-AI solution

Do not manufacture five variants if fewer are genuinely viable.

## 10. Freshness / Maintenance Rules

### Very-high-volatility records
- Gemini preview models
- Grok current model aliases
- DeepSeek preview / current endpoint names
- Kimi current coding variants
- Qwen fast-moving variants
- GLM current 5.x variants
- frontier OpenAI / Claude aliases

### Verify before production use
- exact model name
- API endpoint
- lifecycle status
- context limit
- output limit
- modality support
- tool-use support
- structured-output support
- rate limit
- free tier
- paid pricing
- data retention / training policy
- regional availability
- license
- local hardware requirement
- runtime compatibility

### Historical snapshot rule
- Preserve the original DMS4 2026B snapshot as provenance.
- Do not silently overwrite source-snapshot claims with newer provider claims.
- Add newer metadata as a later verification layer.
- Keep deprecated model IDs resolvable for older QUANDA projects.
- User-facing recommendation text should prefer current verified metadata over dated snapshot metadata.

